[32;1m[1;3mQuestion: Service centres need to be opened at ten candidate locations SC1–SC10 to serve fifteen customers C1–C15. Each centre has a fixed opening cost, and the cost of serving a customer depends on which centre is chosen. The goal is to minimise the sum of all fixed opening costs and customer–centre service costs while ensuring that every customer is served.
service_centers_fixed_costs.csv gives the fixed opening cost for each centre SC1–SC10. expanded_customer_service_costs.csv gives the per-customer service cost from each centre. Row “Customer” is C1–C15; each SC column contains the cost to serve that customer from that centre.
Requirements is that, every customer must be assigned to exactly one service centre. A customer may be assigned only to a centre that is opened. And each opened centre may serve at most 4 customers. Your decision is to choose open or not open each centre and assign each customer to exactly one opened centre, so that to minimise total cost = sum of fixed opening costs for all opened centres + sum of service costs for all customer–centre assignments using the costs in the two CSV files named above.

Thought: I need to retrieve the fixed opening costs for each centre from service_centers_fixed_costs.csv, and the customer–centre service costs from expanded_customer_service_costs.csv. I need to preserve all identifiers (SC1–SC10, C1–C15) and the full cost matrix. The model will use binary variables y_i for opening centre i, and binary variables x_{ij} for assigning customer j to centre i. Each customer must be assigned to exactly one centre, only to an open centre, and each centre can serve at most 4 customers.

Action: CSVQA

Action Input: Retrieve all data from service_centers_fixed_costs.csv and expanded_customer_service_costs.csv, preserving all identifiers and values.
